books/book-06-the-compacts-hand/chapters/chapter-08.md-83-*Third, the integrity provisions themselves,* from Karis's forty pages. Their origin in the auditing of *instruments*: the counsel read the provisions' enacting preamble aloud, which took ninety seconds and which nobody in the room, by the look of the benches, had ever heard read. Their ninety-one recorded invocations, which the researcher had traced individually and tabulated by object — Arbiter frames, sixty-two; station calibration records, nineteen; evaluation protocols and their custody, three — and seven against an individual's conduct, and against what a practitioner *is*, exactly never. And the plain textual fact that a provision written to test whether an instrument could be trusted could not, without violence to its own language, be turned to ask whether a person could be.
