editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-15.md:5:He took the rail at the north end, as he had on the second morning of the year. The high windows were behind him. At this hour the sun came through them low and flat, and laid long bars of light across the painted floor below, over the white crosses and the arrows, so that the whole hall looked ruled like a page in a ledger, with a line for every stride a fighter might take. Lira said she could read it. She said that on a bright morning she could see her own feet a quarter-beat before they landed, because the light told her where the shadow of her heel would fall. She had said it lightly, the way she said most things that mattered to her, and then she had gone out of her way never to train in the afternoon again.
