editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:55:"This house," said Seln. "Our own counter, three weeks ago. A district office asking for the sponsorship papers, *for the proceedings' record*; and in the fourth item, a schedule of every tie this house has to you. Sponsorship, floor hours, the standing that hangs on your evaluation, your travel endorsements." He touched that bean lightly. "The registrar answered it in full, and said in his covering letter that it was in full, twice."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:61:"No," said Seln. He turned his cup a quarter-turn. "That's the way to know it isn't a mistake."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:77:The evening post came up at the eighth bell, and it was Seln who brought the one letter that mattered into the refectory.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:89:Brom laid the sheet down, and nobody said anything, and Seln put his hand in his pocket and took out a fifth bean and set it on the slip, beside the envelope, apart from the others.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:111:"Nearly," said Seln.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:125:"For a ruling." Seln's voice did not change. "Think what five offices' clerks cost for nine weeks. An office spends that only when it already believes it knows the answer. So if the walking has begun, then somebody well above the seat has settled in his mind how the seat will rule, and has started paying for the morning after. The walking is a finding of its own. It's made by people who don't sit in the seat, and made first."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:133:"In the office we called it walking the fences." Seln looked at the five beans. "On paper, it's a pre-restriction inventory."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:155:Cael looked round the table, because looking was what he did when there was nothing to do. Karis with her palm on the notebook's cover. Brom bent forward with his elbows on his knees and his eyes on the board. Lira with the question still lying open where she had put it, and her chin up over it, waiting for one of them to pick it up. And at the middle of the bench Seln, who had walked fences like these himself for years and knew from the inside what the morning after one looked like, sitting with his hands quite still on either side of the five beans, as if the table might tip if he moved them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:157:The others went up in ones and twos, Brom last, with his hand on his coat. Cael stayed. After a while Seln began to put the beans back into his pocket, one at a time, the fifth last.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:227:Then, because Seln had told him to and because he could not have slept otherwise, he wrote the other letter.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:77:The post room was behind the records hall's counter, a narrow whitewashed room with a long sorting bench under the window and pigeonholes from floor to ceiling along the other wall. The district courier's bag came up the hill in the dark and was opened there at first light. Usually the post porter opened it, a broad slow man who had done the sort for twenty years. This morning the post porter was sitting on a stool in the corner with his left arm strapped across his chest, looking unhappy, and the man at the sorting bench with the bag open in front of him was Seln.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:85:The porter had explained, from his stool, before Cael had asked, that he had done his shoulder in lifting a crate on the Fourth-day. The assistant to the assessment wing had come down on the Fifth-day and offered, and had done the sort ever since, and did it very well, though not, the porter said, the way he would have done it himself. Seln did not answer this, or seem to hear it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:87:He sorted the district bag into three piles on the bench. Cael had never watched anybody sort post before with any attention, and he found that it was not one act but four, done so quickly they looked like one. Seln took a letter out of the bag. He read the address. He turned it over and read the seal. He laid it on its pile. He did not hold any letter for longer than another; he did not let his eyes rest on any seal more than on any other. A man watching from the end of the bench would have said he was sorting the post.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:97:He did not say anything. Seln did not say anything. Seln laid another grey seal on Bracken's pile, at exactly the pace he had laid the last one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:99:When the bag was nearly empty, Seln held one letter a moment longer than the others, which he had not done with any letter that morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:161:On the Seventh-day morning Seln came out of the wing at the half-bell before the first, with no papers in his hands at all, and walked across the outer court toward the lodge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:163:Cael was on the covered walk. He had been on the covered walk, in fact, for some time, with the notion that if Seln came out at that hour he might happen to be going the same way. Seln passed him without turning his head. Three strides farther on he said, to the air in front of him, "The lodge. Bring nothing," and kept walking at his even pace.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:169:The lodge was a single room built into the gatehouse wall, with a stove, a high stool, a counter, and a window on the road. The lodge-man was a short square man with a grey beard and a bad knee, who had kept the gate since before Bracken came to the hill. When Seln came in he was already pouring a second cup of tea, and he set it on the counter without being asked and without looking to see who it was for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:177:Seln drank some tea. "I heard he scraped the gatepost."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:181:Seln opened the book at the week's first page and began to read it, and went on listening, and said "Mm" at the right places, while the lodge-man told him the whole of the carter. Then he told him about the carter's father, who had also scraped the gatepost, and what he had said to that one in his day, which had been a good deal more than he had said to the son. It took a quarter of an hour. Cael stood by the stove, where Seln had put him with a glance, and drank the tea the lodge-man had poured him too, and watched.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:183:Seln read the whole week. Arrivals by the day and the bell. Carts, couriers, visitors, deliveries; the four observers who had signed in for the Fourth-day, with their houses beside their names; the correspondents who had been turned back, marked with a small *t* the lodge-man used for *turned*. Then he turned to the back of each day's page, where there was a second, narrow column that Cael had never known existed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:189:Cael read it over Seln's arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:201:Seln shut the book and pushed it back across the counter with a word of thanks. He told the lodge-man that in his opinion the gatepost was in exactly the right place and the carter ought to be made to pay for the stone. The lodge-man said that was what he'd been saying. Seln finished his tea, and set the cup down, and turned it a quarter-turn on the counter before he let go of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:209:Seln walked on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:213:Seln stopped too, a stride ahead, and turned his head a little, not all the way round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:219:Then Seln went on across the court to the wing at his even pace. Cael stood where he was in the cold, with nothing in his hands, and found that he had been taught something, and could not have said at what point in the last quarter hour the teaching had happened.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-16.md:13:Behind the counter, at the copying table, Seln's pen stopped for the length of one word, and went on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-16.md:17:"Mm," said Seln, to his page, and turned it over.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-16.md:167:He had written one before every bout since the mill town: a single line out of the rotation, saying which exchange he would give away, how many bursts he would let the house see, where the spark would go, and what the figure ought to come to at the end. Seln had told him across a document table that his figures were too tidy. The rotation was the cure: scatter, built on purpose and paid for in advance, so that no one with a pencil and a season's worth of his numbers could find the hand that set them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-15.md:143:Seln had heard it before supper, at the copying table.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-15.md:145:Cael had gone round by the assessment wing on his way from the reading room because the lamp was still lit there, and Seln was still at the table behind the counter with the day's last forms, copying. The desk clerk had gone. Cael stood at the counter and gave it to him in a dozen words, the two sentences and who had been refused, and Seln did not look up. He finished the line he was writing. He sanded it. Then he laid the pen down on the blotter, square to the edge, and sat for a moment with his hands in his lap and his eyes on the form in front of him, which was a request for floor hours from a Mire second-year and had nothing to do with anything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-15.md:151:When the plates were mostly empty, at the middle of the bench, Seln turned his cup a quarter-turn on the board.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-15.md:157:Brom put his spoon down and looked at him, the long slow weighing look he gave a load before he got under it, and Seln ate through it without appearing to feel it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-15.md:167:Cael stood on the step and thought about it, and about the voice Seln had said it in, flat, as if the sentence belonged to somebody else; and then about fifteen years of true reports going up a chain to an office that wanted something other than true.

