He thought back. The wing had always had district post. The provision's hours were signed in the wing, and the district seat had written to the wing about them, about observers and schedules and the figures in the wing's book, every few weeks for two years. He had stood at the wing's counter himself and watched the desk clerk open grey seals.

He did not say anything. Seln did not say anything. Seln laid another grey seal on Bracken's pile, at exactly the pace he had laid the last one.

When the bag was nearly empty, Seln held one letter a moment longer than the others, which he had not done with any letter that morning.

"Auremont," he said, and laid it on top of the bundle in Cael's hands, face down, and took out the next.

It had no seal, only a fold tucked in on itself, the way people fold a letter who do not want anybody to think they took trouble over it. Cael did not turn it over in the post room. He carried the house's bundle up to the residence and left it on the hall table. He took the one letter on up the stair and opened it at his own window, where letters were opened in that house, with the light coming up grey over the river and the fountain not yet running below.

*They did this to me the other way — built the ladder to my height and called it fairness. Nobody files challenges against a favorite. Don't mistake what's happening to you for procedure. It stopped being procedure the day they couldn't score you.*

*— D.*

*The rematch stands. Keep yourself unclassified.*

He did not read it again. He got out Hesk's book instead, and a pen, and copied it into the Log word for word, slowly, the way he had once copied the last lines of Gault's note into a letter for Hesk, because copying a thing makes you see every mark in it. Copying, he saw the dash before her initial, and that she had written the last line after the initial and not before it, as if it were an afterthought; and that she had not written *Dear* or *Cael* or anything at all at the top.

He had a piece of her. He went on knowing it the whole time he copied, the way you go on knowing there is a stone in your boot. Somewhere under his ribs, settled since the first morning of the year, sat the weight he had taken from her on the floor at Norhold: the lane, the leaning air, the read that at her weight could have the air or his body and not both. He had paid for it on a stone floor with his whole left side, and he paid for it still, ten counts slow every time he laid a lane. She did not know she had given it. She never would. And here she was in four lines, telling him how to keep what she had never known she was giving away.

Under the copy he wrote one more line.

*From anyone else, a joke. From her, the only one of us who has been classified all the way to the top and lived up there, it's directions. That's a strategy. Filed.*

Then he put the letter itself where nothing else of his lived: inside the back board of Hesk's book, opposite the column, against the page where the costs were kept. It seemed to belong with them. He closed the book on it, and the court below had begun to fill.

---

The evening post came up at the eighth bell, and it was Seln who brought the one letter that mattered into the refectory.

He had been doing the sorts all week for the post porter, mornings and evenings, and he came in from the court with the house's bundle under his arm and one envelope held apart from it in his other hand. He did not give the bundle to anybody. He walked to the middle table, where the four of them were still sitting because none of them had wanted to go up, and he laid the single envelope face up on Karis's blank slip at the end of the row of beans, and stood back.

Cael knew the hand from across the table. Square, upright, the address set out as level as the bubble in a good spirit glass. Denvash.

Nobody touched it for a moment. Then Cael reached over and took it, and broke the fold, and read it where he sat, to himself, all of it, from the brass to the dog to the third paragraph and the single initial at the end. He read the third paragraph twice. Then he did not trust his voice, and he passed the sheet across the table to Brom, with his thumb under the third paragraph.

Brom read it aloud. He read it slowly, the way he read anything, without any change in his voice for any of it.

*Two polite men from the registry came through Denvash asking after my trade licenses and the shop's title. Wanted to know if you had any interest recorded in either. I told them my grandson's interest in this shop is that he grew up in it, and they wrote that down. Whatever they're building, it has a shape. Mind the shape. — H.*

Brom laid the sheet down, and nobody said anything, and Seln put his hand in his pocket and took out a fifth bean and set it on the slip, beside the envelope, apart from the others.

Then Cael took the letter back and read them the rest of it, the first part, the part he had smiled at. He did not quite know why he did it. Perhaps he wanted them to hear Hesk as Hesk was, a man with a bench and a stove and opinions, and not as a line in somebody's inventory.

*The brass came up from the coast at last, and it is a third dearer than last year and not as good, and the founder says it is nobody's fault, which I believe, because it never is. I have a surveyor's level on the bench that came back to me after thirty years. I made it for the man's father. The bubble has wandered a hair, and the son brought it in to be put right; and then, when I had it in pieces, he asked me to set it back the way it was, because his father laid out every boundary in the valley with it a hair wrong and the whole valley is built to agree with it now. I spent four days deciding. In the end I did as he asked.*

*The stove draws well. The shop is the shop. The widow across the lane has a new dog that barks at me in particular, and I have decided to take it as a mark of my character.*
"We won every point that can be won on paper."

Brom took the time he always took. "And?"

"And it's proceeding."

Cael set the night's entry in his head as the wheels found the road. It came out at one line, and he did not try to make it longer.

*Part of me is still keeping score. The rest of me has started counting doors.*

Seln had not opened his eyes since the town. He did not open them now. When he spoke, it was to the whole coach, and not loudly.

"Now you understand the file I've been reading my whole career."

